BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[HEP Seminar] The Dark Dimension\, Revisited — Vincent Lee (UC B
 erkeley / N3AS)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T140000
DTSTAMP:20261013T070000Z
UID:2861@physics.ucsd.edu
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:</b> Vincent Lee (UC Berkeley / N3AS)<br><br>Abstra
 ct: Motivated by the swampland program\, it has been proposed that a tower
  of massive Kaluza-Klein (KK) gravitons from a compactified flat extra dim
 ension can be dark matter\, with the heavier modes constantly cascading to
  lighter ones. The phenomenology crucially depends on the rate at which a 
 massive KK mode decays to lighter modes. We show that\, up to mild assumpt
 ions on the UV theory\, the decay rate is suppressed by a factor of (<i>q<
 /i>/<i>m</i>)^4 compared to the one commonly used in the literature\, wher
 e <i>q</i> is the momentum of the decay products and <i>m</i> is the mass 
 of the decaying KK mode. This suggests that the proposed scenario is ruled
  out by CMB observations by many orders of magnitude. Remarkably\, the sam
 e suppression opens up a vastly different parameter space in which KK grav
 itons from a strongly warped extra dimension (e.g. a Randall-Sundrum setup
 ) are a viable dark matter candidate\, either as the cascading gravitons t
 hemselves or as a stable component of the tower. We discuss the correspond
 ing phenomenology\, and prospects in detecting such dark matter with astro
 physical observations.<br><br><a href="https://physics.ucsd.edu/events/sem
 inars-colloquia">https://physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia</a>
LOCATION:MHA 5623
END:VEVENT
