BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Condense Matter Seminar Series] Fluids within Solids — Fazel Ta
 fti (Boston College)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261202T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261202T170000
DTSTAMP:20261020T070000Z
UID:2878@physics.ucsd.edu
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:</b> Fazel Tafti (Boston College)<br><br><b>Abstrac
 t:</b> I will present recent results from my group that demonstrate fluid-
 like states in solid materials. I start by presenting new advances in find
 ing metallic specimens with extremely large conductivity\, and show how su
 ch materials might enter the hydrostatic limit of electronic transport. I 
 will particularly show our most recent results on NbGe2 \, NbSi2 \, TaGe2 
 \, and TaSi2 that showcase a beautiful example of tuning electron-phonon c
 oupling and reveal its influence on quantum and transport mobility. Next\,
  I will present our recent results in engineering a new generation of Kita
 ev materials\, where bond directional interactions lead to the emergence o
 f a quantum spin liquid with fractionalized anionic particles. I will show
  how physical and chemical pressure have been used to tune the magnetic in
 teractions in these systems.<br><br><b>Speaker Biography:</b> Fazel Tafti 
 completed his PhD at the University of Toronto with Prof. Stephen Julian. 
 His thesis was focused on developing transport experiments under ultrahigh
  pressures in diamond anvil cells. His first postdoc position at the Unive
 rsity of Sherbrooke with Prof. Louis Taillefer was focused on the thermal 
 conductivity and thermoelectric measurements in iron-based superconductors
  under intense magnetic fields. He then changed fields from physics to che
 mistry and completed a second postdoc at Princeton University with Prof. B
 ob Cava. His research was focused on the chemical synthesis of topological
  and magnetic materials. He started his career at Boston College in 2016 w
 here he is currently a tenured associate professor of physics. His lab at 
 BC is interdisciplinary between physics\, chemistry\, and materials scienc
 e.<br><br><a href="https://physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia">htt
 ps://physics.ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia</a>
LOCATION:Mayer Room 4322
END:VEVENT
