BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Condense Matter Seminar Series] What is the dynamical exponent of
  two-dimensional Fermi liquids? — Thomas Scaffidi (UC Irvine)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20270303T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20270303T170000
DTSTAMP:20261014T070000Z
UID:2863@physics.ucsd.edu
DESCRIPTION:<b>Speaker:</b> Thomas Scaffidi (UC Irvine)<br><br>Over the pa
 st decade\, experiments in clean materials have revealed that electrons in
  a solid can flow like a viscous fluid governed by the Navier–Stokes equ
 ations. This raises a fundamental question: can the fermionic nature of el
 ectrons give rise to a different kind of hydrodynamics? The answer turns o
 ut to be yes in two dimensions: long-lived parity-odd deformations of a ci
 rcular Fermi surface act as additional quasi-conserved quantities\, giving
  rise to a distinct\, so-called tomographic regime. In this talk\, I will 
 show how this departure from conventional hydrodynamics extends to the dyn
 amical realm. Starting from a microscopic numerical evaluation of the line
 arized electron–electron collision operator\, we find that the nonlocal 
 conductivity in the tomographic regime is controlled at low frequency by a
  single hydrodynamic pole\, (\\sigma(q\,\\omega)=D(q)/(i\\omega+\\eta_\\st
 ar q^z))\, with dynamical exponent (z=4/3) and superdiffusive viscosity (
 \\eta_\\star). Remarkably\, the Drude weight is itself scale dependent\, o
 beying (D(q)\\sim q^{-\\alpha}) with (\\alpha=1/3)\, so the dynamical resp
 onse is characterized by two distinct exponents rather than one. Finally\,
  I will discuss how AC transport in narrow channels provides a direct expe
 rimental probe of these two scaling laws.<br><br><a href="https://physics.
 ucsd.edu/events/seminars-colloquia">https://physics.ucsd.edu/events/semina
 rs-colloquia</a>
LOCATION:Mayer Room 4322
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
